      "math_academy_id": "q-333304",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below. The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14228295/assets/27e921a76383c4f8a4fc1e81a1febcfad53f330d0e902f4e07578238da33422f.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.07$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.14$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |",
